{
  "dom_id": "step-q93122",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "David saved $$500$ to buy a new cellphone. If he spent only $$420$ to buy the cellphone, what percentage of David's savings did he spend on the phone?",
  "worked_solution": "We're given a part and a whole, and we must find a percentage. So, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=\\text{part}÷\\text{whole}\n$$\n\nIn this case, \n\n- the part is the money David spent, which is $420,$ and\n\n- the whole is the total amount of money saved, which is $500.$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{percentage}=420÷500=\\frac{42}{50}.\n$$\n\nTo write this as a percentage, we create an equivalent fraction with $100$ as the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{50}=\\frac{42\\times 2}{50\\times 2}=\\frac{84}{100}\n$$\n\nNow we can write this fraction as a percentage:\n\n$$\n\\frac{84}{100}=84%\n$$\n\nTherefore, David spent $84%$ of the total money saved.",
